#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529265 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073776 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a24fb4f3-6e3c-5c45-b31e-85b7ce290eda" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Solutions to a System" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050240 :topic/title "Systems of Equations With No Solutions and Infinitely Many Solutions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "344db828-cf79-52e5-a777-8ab84b874966" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a single solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529268} {:answer/id #uuid "95396471-ccc2-5ee9-a8d6-43bc15e844c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529298} {:answer/id #uuid "dcdd0cde-b36f-54e5-a018-7db85e9b5a1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "344db828-cf79-52e5-a777-8ab84b874966" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a single solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529268} :answer-field/id #uuid "65b4752f-c56d-5617-8e96-0a2d7a4fd12a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529281}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "27a6ba63-3c85-5feb-bfac-b122a5e9f9a3" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$\\{-4x + 4y = -9; 2x - y = -4$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the second equation by $2$, then we will get a $4x$ term that can cancel with the $-4x$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\{-4x + 4y = -9; 2x - y = -4 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (2)\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, the $x$ terms cancel, and we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-4x + 4y &= -9 \\\\\n4x - 2y &= -8 \\\\\n2y &= (-9 - 8) \\\\\n2y &= -17 \\\\\ny &= - \\frac{17}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-4x + (4)(- \\frac{17}{2}) &= -9 \\\\\n-4x &= 25 \\\\\nx &= - \\frac{25}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system has *a single solution*, $(- \\frac{25}{4},- \\frac{17}{2})$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529266 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073776 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a24fb4f3-6e3c-5c45-b31e-85b7ce290eda" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Solutions to a System" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050240 :topic/title "Systems of Equations With No Solutions and Infinitely Many Solutions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fbdb8ad-267a-57e1-90cb-804824fd158a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a single solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529259} {:answer/id #uuid "31e0880c-27b5-5a13-87e6-3705af860da9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529267} {:answer/id #uuid "8c8210fd-75fb-5771-887f-af1bb8ec8244" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529295}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c8210fd-75fb-5771-887f-af1bb8ec8244" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529295} :answer-field/id #uuid "6093ddec-b3cd-5a0b-8a78-bde2db784aa4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "31a5b8b3-2787-5aca-9212-729c0bca425f" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$\\{y + 2x + 2 = 0; 4y + 8x + 8 = 0$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nFirst, rearrange each equation to put the variable terms on the left and the constant on the right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + y &= -2 \\\\\n8x + 4y &= -8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $-4$, then we will get a $-8x$ term that can cancel with the $8x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{2x + y = -2 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (-4); 8x + 4y = -8\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-8x - 4y &= 8 \\\\\n8x + 4y &= -8 \\\\\n0 &= (8 - 8) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe statement $0=0$ is *always true*. Therefore, we conclude that the system has *infinitely many solutions*."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529271 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073776 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a24fb4f3-6e3c-5c45-b31e-85b7ce290eda" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Solutions to a System" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050240 :topic/title "Systems of Equations With No Solutions and Infinitely Many Solutions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "193a9776-9522-59e8-93fb-4b3bab86a5ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529262} {:answer/id #uuid "ac9317b7-c236-5932-99c1-b650c3ecfd60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a single solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529311} {:answer/id #uuid "ece64018-7584-599e-8625-2cc9d1912aa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ece64018-7584-599e-8625-2cc9d1912aa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529327} :answer-field/id #uuid "e77cb455-ca55-5d77-ab3a-caef6fb910d6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529326}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "45038183-ddb8-5749-92cf-2124aeefd6a9" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$\\{2x + y = 2; 4x + 2y = 5$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $-2$, then we will get a $-4x$ term that can cancel with the $4x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{2x + y = 2 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (-2); 4x + 2y = 5\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-4x - 2y &= -4 \\\\\n4x + 2y &= 5 \\\\\n0 &= (-4 + 5) \\\\\n0 &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe statement $0=1$ is *always false*. Therefore, we conclude that the system has *no solutions*."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529284 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073776 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a24fb4f3-6e3c-5c45-b31e-85b7ce290eda" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Solutions to a System" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050240 :topic/title "Systems of Equations With No Solutions and Infinitely Many Solutions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a6a9c04-4483-5b31-88cd-fdc0d4f8dfab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529273} {:answer/id #uuid "6532299d-7242-529d-b584-f2d02d122b29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529280} {:answer/id #uuid "a3bb91a8-f370-546d-8650-fc4b5ac0f2bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a single solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529304}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3bb91a8-f370-546d-8650-fc4b5ac0f2bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a single solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529304} :answer-field/id #uuid "571d0d37-8178-57f9-8598-a1a4ba418722" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529277}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6df7ea41-de66-50d4-b68f-ac8978094097" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$\\{y - 3x + 1 = 0; -3y - 6x - 8 = 0$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nFirst, rearrange each equation to put the variable terms on the left and the constant on the right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3x + y &= -1 \\\\\n-6x - 3y &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $-2$, then we will get a $6x$ term that can cancel with the $-6x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{-3x + y = -1 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (-2); -6x - 3y = 8\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, the $x$ terms cancel, and we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6x - 2y &= 2 \\\\\n-6x - 3y &= 8 \\\\\n-5y &= (2 + 8) \\\\\n-5y &= 10 \\\\\ny &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3x + (1)(-2) &= -1 \\\\\n-3x &= 1 \\\\\nx &= - \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system has *a single solution*, $(- \\frac{1}{3},-2)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529290 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073776 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a24fb4f3-6e3c-5c45-b31e-85b7ce290eda" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Solutions to a System" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050240 :topic/title "Systems of Equations With No Solutions and Infinitely Many Solutions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46de5fcd-a47b-5970-9703-3a33b936c273" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529272} {:answer/id #uuid "9f4a900e-af83-5010-a4eb-550d23e0386f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a single solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529302} {:answer/id #uuid "c4bc71c3-edaf-56da-873d-7ff9f6b2544c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f4a900e-af83-5010-a4eb-550d23e0386f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a single solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529302} :answer-field/id #uuid "defa217b-2cb1-5a93-8730-0a2de2c923fb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529325}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b1e2d9a-93ed-5edd-af89-794105a0828b" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$\\{2x - 3y = 10; 2x - y = 6$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $-1$, then we will get a $-2x$ term that can cancel with the $2x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{2x - 3y = 10 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (-1); 2x - y = 6\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, the $x$ terms cancel, and we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2x + 3y &= -10 \\\\\n2x - y &= 6 \\\\\n2y &= (-10 + 6) \\\\\n2y &= -4 \\\\\ny &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + (-3)(-2) &= 10 \\\\\n2x &= 4 \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system has *a single solution*, $(2,-2)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529293 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073776 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a24fb4f3-6e3c-5c45-b31e-85b7ce290eda" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Solutions to a System" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050240 :topic/title "Systems of Equations With No Solutions and Infinitely Many Solutions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d893df7-d857-5192-90ce-47a99d5c6bf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529274} {:answer/id #uuid "60bc4533-01cd-595e-9372-f185d24aa9f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a single solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529279} {:answer/id #uuid "6ce6a4fc-25b3-542c-aed5-70e5fdd4a918" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529283}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d893df7-d857-5192-90ce-47a99d5c6bf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529274} :answer-field/id #uuid "7aa9bc9c-c996-5d30-bb22-558885c83ec4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529289}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "878a99ca-9540-5195-b258-8e98913442f4" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$\\{2x - y = -2; 8x - 4y = -8$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $-4$, then we will get a $-8x$ term that can cancel with the $8x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{2x - y = -2 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (-4); 8x - 4y = -8\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-8x + 4y &= 8 \\\\\n8x - 4y &= -8 \\\\\n0 &= (8 - 8) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe statement $0=0$ is *always true*. Therefore, we conclude that the system has *infinitely many solutions*."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529294 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073776 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a24fb4f3-6e3c-5c45-b31e-85b7ce290eda" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Solutions to a System" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050240 :topic/title "Systems of Equations With No Solutions and Infinitely Many Solutions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aedf24db-2200-5c34-82c8-69e05734e790" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529313} {:answer/id #uuid "d688773d-636f-5a54-b4ee-355c9f396330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529321} {:answer/id #uuid "f2dee143-55db-5cf5-83a5-24cd943aae58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a single solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529329}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2dee143-55db-5cf5-83a5-24cd943aae58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a single solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529329} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5171cd4-7721-5fe2-926b-265c33c00b33" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "88e09a62-e93a-583d-86c5-5caff22bdcbd" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$\\{3x + y = 0; -3x + 5y = -2$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $1$, then we will get a $3x$ term that can cancel with the $-3x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{3x + y = 0 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (1); -3x + 5y = -2\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, the $x$ terms cancel, and we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x + y &= 0 \\\\\n-3x + 5y &= -2 \\\\\n6y &= (0 - 2) \\\\\n6y &= -2 \\\\\ny &= - \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x + (1)(- \\frac{1}{3}) &= 0 \\\\\n3x &= \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{1}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system has *a single solution*, $(\\frac{1}{9},- \\frac{1}{3})$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529306 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073776 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a24fb4f3-6e3c-5c45-b31e-85b7ce290eda" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Solutions to a System" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050240 :topic/title "Systems of Equations With No Solutions and Infinitely Many Solutions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26412d41-7859-567a-8d0b-616fdfaae78b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a single solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529264} {:answer/id #uuid "7ebeb28b-d7e8-58ed-a1ac-c3da8dc6de13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529291} {:answer/id #uuid "ede703e4-312f-541c-9af9-a20919d7afce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ebeb28b-d7e8-58ed-a1ac-c3da8dc6de13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529291} :answer-field/id #uuid "43939bab-211c-5b1e-9dcd-127b5f8c732a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529270}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a7d7084c-4c14-5312-a22d-dc6fe1986a2c" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$\\{-3x - y = 8; 3x + y = -6$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $1$, then we will get a $-3x$ term that can cancel with the $3x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{-3x - y = 8 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (1); 3x + y = -6\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3x - y &= 8 \\\\\n3x + y &= -6 \\\\\n0 &= (8 - 6) \\\\\n0 &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe statement $0=2$ is *always false*. Therefore, we conclude that the system has *no solutions*."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529307 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073776 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a24fb4f3-6e3c-5c45-b31e-85b7ce290eda" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Solutions to a System" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050240 :topic/title "Systems of Equations With No Solutions and Infinitely Many Solutions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d5289c2-ae65-5854-8e71-5b033554cd5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529258} {:answer/id #uuid "81a01f27-1606-571b-a96b-5a5040320fbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529292} {:answer/id #uuid "9d492ab7-1bb8-5fc1-8c03-1d65e9d953d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a single solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d492ab7-1bb8-5fc1-8c03-1d65e9d953d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a single solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529301} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2ae9c90-b5af-556d-b9c0-2e84879f0db0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529303}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a81cacd7-ddb8-5f18-91b0-0d7142c96649" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$\\{y - 3x - 4 = 0; -2y + 12x + 10 = 0$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nFirst, rearrange each equation to put the variable terms on the left and the constant on the right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3x + y &= 4 \\\\\n12x - 2y &= -10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $4$, then we will get a $-12x$ term that can cancel with the $12x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{-3x + y = 4 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (4); 12x - 2y = -10\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, the $x$ terms cancel, and we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-12x + 4y &= 16 \\\\\n12x - 2y &= -10 \\\\\n2y &= (16 - 10) \\\\\n2y &= 6 \\\\\ny &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3x + (1)(3) &= 4 \\\\\n-3x &= 1 \\\\\nx &= - \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system has *a single solution*, $(- \\frac{1}{3},3)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529309 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073776 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a24fb4f3-6e3c-5c45-b31e-85b7ce290eda" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Solutions to a System" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050240 :topic/title "Systems of Equations With No Solutions and Infinitely Many Solutions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02e4adab-f965-574b-b1b2-5412f0ac4030" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a single solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529257} {:answer/id #uuid "174a23c4-66b4-574b-ab16-58c635103256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529260} {:answer/id #uuid "dbf0dc60-cd74-50bb-bbf6-488567b34736" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529323}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "174a23c4-66b4-574b-ab16-58c635103256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529260} :answer-field/id #uuid "da21e2ad-53b7-539d-8fc6-d73ab8480a65" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a93a39cc-73b6-5e4b-80a4-1282a4d64974" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$\\{y + 3x + 6 = 0; -4y - 12x - 26 = 0$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nFirst, rearrange each equation to put the variable terms on the left and the constant on the right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x + y &= -6 \\\\\n-12x - 4y &= 26\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $4$, then we will get a $12x$ term that can cancel with the $-12x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{3x + y = -6 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (4); -12x - 4y = 26\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12x + 4y &= -24 \\\\\n-12x - 4y &= 26 \\\\\n0 &= (-24 + 26) \\\\\n0 &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe statement $0=2$ is *always false*. Therefore, we conclude that the system has *no solutions*."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529316 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073776 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a24fb4f3-6e3c-5c45-b31e-85b7ce290eda" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Solutions to a System" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050240 :topic/title "Systems of Equations With No Solutions and Infinitely Many Solutions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72bba841-9529-5598-b921-f90b8dffbe5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529286} {:answer/id #uuid "b27078db-ea72-5bd8-9115-2ea5343e2f7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a single solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529315} {:answer/id #uuid "f96540d7-0eb1-5d77-a5ce-3fa81473a266" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529331}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b27078db-ea72-5bd8-9115-2ea5343e2f7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a single solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529315} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c1fbec3-c232-5078-a580-2f08b5aa37e3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c0b2af50-0676-5686-aa21-aed280aaa725" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$\\{-x - 5y = -12; x - 3y = -2$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $1$, then we will get a $-x$ term that can cancel with the $x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{-x - 5y = -12 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (1); x - 3y = -2\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, the $x$ terms cancel, and we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x - 5y &= -12 \\\\\nx - 3y &= -2 \\\\\n-8y &= (-12 - 2) \\\\\n-8y &= -14 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{7}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x + (-5)(\\frac{7}{4}) &= -12 \\\\\n-x &= - \\frac{13}{4} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{13}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system has *a single solution*, $(\\frac{13}{4},\\frac{7}{4})$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529318 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073776 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a24fb4f3-6e3c-5c45-b31e-85b7ce290eda" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Solutions to a System" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050240 :topic/title "Systems of Equations With No Solutions and Infinitely Many Solutions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f5d6e30-8e43-5909-9e9a-3a9af82fd1e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a single solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529297} {:answer/id #uuid "9c1af611-f1dc-57a7-b4ff-1cd30b5d58d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529300} {:answer/id #uuid "a979b510-0d7a-5573-bce5-5f77d85dffa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529310}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f5d6e30-8e43-5909-9e9a-3a9af82fd1e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a single solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529297} :answer-field/id #uuid "8eaf5b55-2fa9-5c35-a346-66798bb8f61b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529296}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c5970b8b-793c-585b-9c66-c8937f9714fc" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$\\{x - 3y = -6; 4x - y = -12$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $-4$, then we will get a $-4x$ term that can cancel with the $4x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{x - 3y = -6 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (-4); 4x - y = -12\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, the $x$ terms cancel, and we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-4x + 12y &= 24 \\\\\n4x - y &= -12 \\\\\n11y &= (24 - 12) \\\\\n11y &= 12 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{12}{11}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + (-3)(\\frac{12}{11}) &= -6 \\\\\nx &= - \\frac{30}{11} \\\\\nx &= - \\frac{30}{11}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system has *a single solution*, $(- \\frac{30}{11},\\frac{12}{11})$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529320 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073776 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a24fb4f3-6e3c-5c45-b31e-85b7ce290eda" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Solutions to a System" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050240 :topic/title "Systems of Equations With No Solutions and Infinitely Many Solutions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fb0b299-f16e-54c2-a393-40d6eef9a51c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529276} {:answer/id #uuid "97b495fd-486c-5329-aea6-b7fef9362dc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a single solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529299} {:answer/id #uuid "a66f4ab0-02ae-5f53-884d-c689e9f292e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529305}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a66f4ab0-02ae-5f53-884d-c689e9f292e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "infinitely many solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529305} :answer-field/id #uuid "766ff9b6-bfc6-59ad-af1f-7918abd24f91" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529287}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d4bc3484-819e-515d-b935-6bb887858fab" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$\\{2x - y = 4; 2x - y = 4$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $-1$, then we will get a $-2x$ term that can cancel with the $2x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{2x - y = 4 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (-1); 2x - y = 4\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2x + y &= -4 \\\\\n2x - y &= 4 \\\\\n0 &= (-4 + 4) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe statement $0=0$ is *always true*. Therefore, we conclude that the system has *infinitely many solutions*."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542434 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50351666-c691-510f-863c-168c61f9980b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System With Infinitely Many Intersection Points" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050240 :topic/title "Systems of Equations With No Solutions and Infinitely Many Solutions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "296b4bc1-fdc9-57c7-8838-c40039d81743" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(u, -4u + 8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542454} {:answer/id #uuid "511c3e07-7b86-5d35-b593-e086d056c9e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(u, -\\frac{1}{4}u + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542467} {:answer/id #uuid "72f94d33-8b49-509e-9f3b-feef2915a2d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(u, 8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542474} {:answer/id #uuid "993d12f6-8426-5581-93dc-e143dd73cada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(u, 4u + 8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542483} {:answer/id #uuid "d647aaba-1ddd-551d-acf3-4af820d6199d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(u, -4u - 8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "296b4bc1-fdc9-57c7-8838-c40039d81743" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(u, -4u + 8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542454} :answer-field/id #uuid "a496eb60-5280-58e8-963e-50a6d3c4762a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542486}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "088c2bc7-791c-5d33-989e-2db1686f3a99" :question/problem "Find all points of intersection of the lines $4x + y = 8$ and $-8x - 2y =-16$." :question/worked-solution "The points of intersection of the lines correspond to the solutions of the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x + y &=8 \\\\ -8x - 2y &=-16\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using elimination. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $2$, then we will get a $8x$ term that can cancel with the $-8x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x + y &=8 && \\mid \\mid \\cdot (2) \\\\ -8x - 2y &=-16\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8x + 2y &= 16 \\\\\n-8x - 2y &= -16 \\\\\n0 &= (16 - 16) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the lines have infinitely many intersection points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every solution to the system. Any value of $x$ can be chosen, and we can find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x + y &= 8 \\\\\ny &= 8 - 4x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{8 - 4x}{1} \\\\\ny &= 8 - 4x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=x$ be the free coordinate.\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(u,8 - 4u \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is any number."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542435 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50351666-c691-510f-863c-168c61f9980b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System With Infinitely Many Intersection Points" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050240 :topic/title "Systems of Equations With No Solutions and Infinitely Many Solutions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76fe33fa-ce38-5b2f-ae1b-8a6fb1ff5121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(u, \\frac{1}{4}u + \\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542475} {:answer/id #uuid "c99c9047-694a-52fb-90a1-920055039ded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(u, \\frac{1}{4}u - \\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542494} {:answer/id #uuid "ca2b29a2-d8c3-5c8b-ab33-30360a9a3463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(u, -\\frac{1}{4}u - \\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542495} {:answer/id #uuid "e3aad1d2-6e42-5eec-9dca-2cec7a5ae0b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(u, 4u + 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542503} {:answer/id #uuid "ff7fd8bf-f7d2-584f-a1df-b2f38182412f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(u, -\\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542511}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c99c9047-694a-52fb-90a1-920055039ded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(u, \\frac{1}{4}u - \\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542494} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bf547af-6a9e-5a7b-a100-571509ee17b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0b630dc7-b870-5e2b-85ac-37faee98683d" :question/problem "Find all points of intersection of the lines $x - 4y = 3$ and $-4x + 16y =-12$." :question/worked-solution "The points of intersection of the lines correspond to the solutions of the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x - 4y &=3 \\\\ -4x + 16y &=-12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using elimination. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $4$, then we will get a $4x$ term that can cancel with the $-4x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x - 4y &=3 && \\mid \\mid \\cdot (4) \\\\ -4x + 16y &=-12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x - 16y &= 12 \\\\\n-4x + 16y &= -12 \\\\\n0 &= (12 - 12) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the lines have infinitely many intersection points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every solution to the system. Any value of $x$ can be chosen, and we can find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 4y &= 3 \\\\\n-4y &= 3 - x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{3 - x}{-4} \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{3}{4} + \\frac{1}{4}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=x$ be the free coordinate.\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(u,-\\frac{3}{4} + \\frac{1}{4}u \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is any number."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50351666-c691-510f-863c-168c61f9980b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System With Infinitely Many Intersection Points" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050240 :topic/title "Systems of Equations With No Solutions and Infinitely Many Solutions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a28e7ec-e71a-5f77-bd2b-aee856b5039c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(t, 2t + 10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542444} {:answer/id #uuid "1bbc4b58-5af2-531c-9f2a-e95c4507960c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(t, \\frac{1}{2}t - 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542446} {:answer/id #uuid "2528686b-8db2-507f-9663-1012eeb1fbf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(t, -5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542453} {:answer/id #uuid "6ae1c305-4510-5421-8776-69299ea2148b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(t, -\\frac{1}{2}t - 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542473} {:answer/id #uuid "ea2d669d-5e96-546b-9cbb-0faab3e8ff37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(t, \\frac{1}{2}t + 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bbc4b58-5af2-531c-9f2a-e95c4507960c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(t, \\frac{1}{2}t - 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542446} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f8a0b2e-30d5-55be-9faa-704ff5f10ec6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "111bc200-5458-59e4-8bff-0e4766f93526" :question/problem "Find all points of intersection of the lines $x - 2y = 10$ and $3x - 6y = 30$." :question/worked-solution "The points of intersection of the lines correspond to the solutions of the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x - 2y &=10 \\\\ 3x - 6y &=30\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using elimination. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $-3$, then we will get a $-3x$ term that can cancel with the $3x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x - 2y &=10 && \\mid \\mid \\cdot (-3) \\\\ 3x - 6y &=30\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3x + 6y &= -30 \\\\\n3x - 6y &= 30 \\\\\n0 &= (-30 + 30) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the lines have infinitely many intersection points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every solution to the system. Any value of $x$ can be chosen, and we can find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 2y &= 10 \\\\\n-2y &= 10 - x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{10 - x}{-2} \\\\\ny &= -5 + \\frac{1}{2}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $t=x$ be the free coordinate.\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(t,-5 + \\frac{1}{2}t \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is any number."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542448 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50351666-c691-510f-863c-168c61f9980b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System With Infinitely Many Intersection Points" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050240 :topic/title "Systems of Equations With No Solutions and Infinitely Many Solutions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "216e4d60-197f-5cc4-a6a9-1f0aa4798fd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(u, -1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542451} {:answer/id #uuid "677f2479-49ce-5f74-a164-ff8f5c291ff1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(u, -2u - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542472} {:answer/id #uuid "925e9f49-23ce-555d-bfa2-ceaec62717c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(u, 2u + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542481} {:answer/id #uuid "b93fa3dc-f26f-535d-b424-a60e27978200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(u, 2u - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542490} {:answer/id #uuid "f9ce12fe-1da9-5977-a390-7992e5b701d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(u, \\frac{1}{2}u + \\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542508}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b93fa3dc-f26f-535d-b424-a60e27978200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(u, 2u - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542490} :answer-field/id #uuid "db9b9d32-7ef3-5e9b-8814-93028e12c1b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1dff70b5-3044-54d1-b535-83ffa052215f" :question/problem "Find all points of intersection of the lines $2x - y = 1$ and $-4x + 2y =-2$." :question/worked-solution "The points of intersection of the lines correspond to the solutions of the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x - y &=1 \\\\ -4x + 2y &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using elimination. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $2$, then we will get a $4x$ term that can cancel with the $-4x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x - y &=1 && \\mid \\mid \\cdot (2) \\\\ -4x + 2y &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x - 2y &= 2 \\\\\n-4x + 2y &= -2 \\\\\n0 &= (2 - 2) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the lines have infinitely many intersection points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every solution to the system. Any value of $x$ can be chosen, and we can find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x - y &= 1 \\\\\n-y &= 1 - 2x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{1 - 2x}{-1} \\\\\ny &= -1 + 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=x$ be the free coordinate.\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(u,-1 + 2u \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is any number."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542452 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50351666-c691-510f-863c-168c61f9980b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System With Infinitely Many Intersection Points" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050240 :topic/title "Systems of Equations With No Solutions and Infinitely Many Solutions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "329c8c6c-64c9-53b9-abcb-a88c8eeeb32e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t + 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542458}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "329c8c6c-64c9-53b9-abcb-a88c8eeeb32e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t + 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542458} :answer-field/id #uuid "d774ba4e-5e04-5b25-ac80-8bb3704ee4df" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542498}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "23da70bf-d870-5a7b-895c-27d6ebda3437" :question/problem "Describe the points that lie on the intersection of the lines $2x - y =-6$ and $6x - 3y =-18$. Express your answer in terms of $t$ only.\n\nThe intersection points are $(t$,\n{{field-1}}\n$)$, where $t$ is any number." :question/worked-solution "The points of intersection of the lines correspond to the solutions of the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x - y &=-6 \\\\ 6x - 3y &=-18\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using elimination. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $-3$, then we will get a $-6x$ term that can cancel with the $6x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x - y &=-6 && \\mid \\mid \\cdot (-3) \\\\ 6x - 3y &=-18\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-6x + 3y &= 18 \\\\\n6x - 3y &= -18 \\\\\n0 &= (18 - 18) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the lines have infinitely many intersection points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every solution to the system. Any value of $x$ can be chosen, and we can find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x - y &= -6 \\\\\n-y &= -6 - 2x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{-6 - 2x}{-1} \\\\\ny &= 6 + 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $t=x$ be the free coordinate.\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(t,6 + 2t \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is any number."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542462 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50351666-c691-510f-863c-168c61f9980b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System With Infinitely Many Intersection Points" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050240 :topic/title "Systems of Equations With No Solutions and Infinitely Many Solutions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b794602-b4c5-54ab-8bfc-db1e61594521" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x + \\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542479}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b794602-b4c5-54ab-8bfc-db1e61594521" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x + \\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542479} :answer-field/id #uuid "337c5621-421b-56d4-95de-5c351f0f1ad9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542460}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3e38b0b3-85f1-537d-9bdc-738a5616e813" :question/problem "Describe the points that lie on the intersection of the lines $4x - 2y =-11$ and $8x - 4y =-22$. Express your answer in terms of $x$ only.\n\nThe intersection points are $(x$,\n{{field-1}}\n$)$, where $x$ is any number." :question/worked-solution "The points of intersection of the lines correspond to the solutions of the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x - 2y &=-11 \\\\ 8x - 4y &=-22\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using elimination. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $-2$, then we will get a $-8x$ term that can cancel with the $8x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x - 2y &=-11 && \\mid \\mid \\cdot (-2) \\\\ 8x - 4y &=-22\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-8x + 4y &= 22 \\\\\n8x - 4y &= -22 \\\\\n0 &= (22 - 22) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the lines have infinitely many intersection points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every solution to the system. Any value of $x$ can be chosen, and we can find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x - 2y &= -11 \\\\\n-2y &= -11 - 4x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{-11 - 4x}{-2} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{11}{2} + 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(x,\\frac{11}{2} + 2x \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is any number."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542465 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50351666-c691-510f-863c-168c61f9980b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System With Infinitely Many Intersection Points" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050240 :topic/title "Systems of Equations With No Solutions and Infinitely Many Solutions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1265d20a-0e86-57c1-9549-b3dffb34e245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{5}x - \\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1265d20a-0e86-57c1-9549-b3dffb34e245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{5}x - \\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542443} :answer-field/id #uuid "0eef6127-aaa5-5be2-ac0e-d2adcd5bbbc7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542439}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "48520d96-a4e1-575b-9ec9-8c8c3ed1fafc" :question/problem "Describe the points that lie on the intersection of the lines $-12x + 5y =-6$ and $24x - 10y = 12$. Express your answer in terms of $x$ only.\n\nThe intersection points are $(x$,\n{{field-1}}\n$)$, where $x$ is any number." :question/worked-solution "The points of intersection of the lines correspond to the solutions of the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-12x + 5y &=-6 \\\\ 24x - 10y &=12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using elimination. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $2$, then we will get a $-24x$ term that can cancel with the $24x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-12x + 5y &=-6 && \\mid \\mid \\cdot (2) \\\\ 24x - 10y &=12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-24x + 10y &= -12 \\\\\n24x - 10y &= 12 \\\\\n0 &= (-12 + 12) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the lines have infinitely many intersection points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every solution to the system. Any value of $x$ can be chosen, and we can find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-12x + 5y &= -6 \\\\\n5y &= -6 + 12x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{-6 + 12x}{5} \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{6}{5} + \\frac{12}{5}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(x,-\\frac{6}{5} + \\frac{12}{5}x \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is any number."}]}
